<header><p class="enc-kicker">Person</p><h1 class="enc-title">Mausolus</h1><p class="enc-meta"><span class="dates">d. 353 BCE</span> · <a href="/centuries/4th-century-bce">4th century BCE</a></p></header>
<div class="enc-body"><p class="enc-desc">Mausolus was the Hecatomnid ruler of Caria from 377 to 353 BCE, formally a Persian <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/satrapy">satrap</a> but in practice an independent dynast. He moved his capital to Halicarnassus, which he enlarged and fortified, and extended his power over Lycia and the nearby Greek islands. His monumental tomb, completed by his wife Artemisia II, gave the word mausoleum and was a Wonder of the World.</p><figure class="enc-fig"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/mausolus.jpg" alt="Mausolus" width="320" height="355" fetchpriority="high"><figcaption>Image: shakko · CC BY-SA 3.0 · <a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Mausolus_portrait.jpg" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Wikimedia Commons</a></figcaption></figure></div>
<section class="enc-quotes"><h2 class="enc-section-title">Across the Institute</h2><ul><li><blockquote>In the fourth century it regained its fleet and freedom, leaving the Second Athenian League in the Social War of 357–355 BCE with the support of <b>Mausolus</b> of Caria, but soon fell under the influence of Carian dynasts and Persian-backed oligarchs.</blockquote><a href="/empire/chios">Chios · Decline &amp; Fall</a></li><li><blockquote><b>Mausolus of Hecatomnid Caria</b> made Halicarnassus his capital in the 370s BCE, absorbing it into his dynastic state; his tomb, the Mausoleum, was a Wonder of the World.</blockquote><a href="/state/halicarnassus">Halicarnassus</a></li><li><blockquote>His son <b>Mausolus</b> moved the capital to Halicarnassus, extended control over Lycia and the islands, and combined Carian and Greek culture.</blockquote><a href="/state/hecatomnid-caria">Hecatomnid Caria</a></li><li><blockquote>In the 4th century BCE Pericles of Limyra briefly united Lycia and joined the revolt against Persia, but <b>Mausolus of Hecatomnid Caria</b> took control c. 360 BCE.</blockquote><a href="/state/lycian-dynasts">Lycian dynasts</a></li><li><blockquote>In the Social War of 357-355 BCE Chios, Rhodes, Cos and Byzantium, encouraged by <b>Mausolus</b> of Caria, left the league.</blockquote><a href="/state/second-athenian-league">Second Athenian League</a></li></ul></section>
